AB  - Hydraulic efficiency in free water surface (FWS) constructed wetlands can be reduced by obstructions, sedimentation, and microtopographic variability, which disrupt flow paths and create dead zones. This study extends a standard 2D depth-averaged hydraulic model with slope-sensitive roughness. The formulation enables spatially explicit simulation of terrain-driven resistance. The framework is computationally efficient, modular, and reproducible. It enables rapid testing of alternative configurations under diverse boundary conditions. We simulated synthetic scenarios with varied obstructions (sediment, vegetation) and bed topographies. This evaluated their impact on flow distribution, residence time, and efficiency. Inlet obstructions and clustered vegetation reduced retention. Lateral vegetation preserved flow corridors. Transverse topography enhanced mixing. Staged gradients improved retention. Excessive or chaotic roughness fragmented circulation and promoted early breakthrough. This modeling approach is transferable. It evaluates wetland hydraulic function and supports design optimization.
