AB  - Low-cost sensors (LCS), such as compact electrochemical and optical probes for monitoring water-quality indicators, enable rapid water-quality data collection but face deployment challenges due to high uncertainty. This study proposes a hierarchical framework integrating quality–quantity analysis, Value of Information (VOI), and complex network refinement. In Stage I, an optimal sampling density grade (hereafter referred to as grade, a classified discrete grade characterizing sampling intensity, unitless) is selected via mutual information-confidence interval (MI–CI) analysis. A subsequent Local Indicators of Spatial Association (LISA)-based adjustment refines the spatial distribution of candidate sites, yielding the final deployment scale N∗. In Stage II, candidate sites are prioritized using VOI, with coverage and structural gains as modifiers. Validated on Lake Taihu, China, across multiple periods and uncertainty scenarios, the framework improves hotspot identification compared to expert-based methods. By decoupling grade selection from spatial prioritization, it provides a transparent approach for robust LCS deployment under uncertainty.
