AB  - Positional uncertainty in species occurrence records refers to the deviation of geographic coordinates from their true locations. Such uncertainty can reduce both the predictive accuracy and ecological interpretability of species distribution models. We proposed a simulated annealing optimization framework that iteratively adjusts geographic coordinates to maximize model performance and the ecological plausibility of response curves, which can be flexibly embedded into different species distribution models. Using MaxEnt (Maximum entropy model) as a case study, we validated the framework on Ailuropoda melanoleuca (A. melanoleuca) and virtual species datasets. The results demonstrate that coordinates optimization significantly enhances the discriminative capacity of multiple species distribution models. Additionally, the characteristics of the morphology of the primary response curves are enhanced. Predictions from optimized lower-precision data show minimal differences compared with predictions based on high-precision data. This approach provides a feasible solution to reduce positional uncertainty in species distribution modeling.
