AB  - Wind-field simulation over complex terrain requires terrain meshes that capture local topographical variations while remaining suitable for numerical preprocessing. This study proposes a geometry-aware terrain mesh optimization framework coupling Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) with Optimal Delaunay Triangulation (ODT). To improve search efficiency, the three-dimensional terrain optimization problem is reduced to a two-dimensional one: terrain points are projected onto a plane, optimized in the projected space, and reconstructed in three-dimensional space using Kriging interpolation. A terrain-density weight is embedded into the two-dimensional ODT energy to introduce salient three-dimensional terrain information and guide local mesh redistribution. The method was evaluated using aspect ratio, radius ratio, minimum angle, and a comparative downstream CFD case. The results showed improved mesh regularity and locally refined terrain representation while maintaining generally consistent main flow-field patterns, indicating its potential as a reproducible preprocessing strategy for complex-terrain wind-field simulations.
