AB  - Modeling pollutant diffusion remains a central challenge in environmental science due to multi-scale processes, incomplete observations and uncertain boundary or initial conditions. We observe close conceptual parallels between generative diffusion models and physical diffusion processes, motivating the adaptation of generative diffusion methods for environmental modeling. We target four canonical tasks: spatiotemporal forecasting, spatial downscaling, data assimilation, and emission/parameter inversion. We provide a systematic mapping between pollutant transport physics and generative diffusion principles, and survey recent applications of diffusion models in environmental contexts. Based on this analysis, we propose four task-specific frameworks—Time-aligned, Corrective, Autoregressive and Deterministic Diffusion—that integrate physical constraints and uncertainty quantification into generative models. We provide conceptual framework prototypes that illustrate how generative diffusion models can achieve physically consistent probabilistic forecasting, bias-corrected downscaling, stable data assimilation, and robust inversion. This work establishes a principled pathway to couple generative diffusion with environmental physics for future empirical and operational studies.
