AB  - Environmental modelling involves many decisions, ranging from choices about scope and boundaries, and which system components to represent, to determining how model results are communicated. However, many of these decisions are not systematically considered, which can undermine the credibility and effectiveness of models. This study introduces a structured framework that considers choices at decision forks in the modelling cycle. We suggest using the concept of fitness-for-purpose and a pathway perspective to reflect on modelling decisions through four steps: a) identification of decision boundaries; b) modelling pathway and reasoning; c) evaluation of modelling decisions; and d) iterative refinement. This approach aims to enhance transparency and effectiveness, going beyond documentation of model design and testing by explicitly documenting the rationale and reasons for each modelling decision. We demonstrate the use of this framework for the case of an eco-hydrological model that simulates vegetation condition to support environmental flow management in the Narran Lakes, Australia.
