AB  - Floods cause significant damage globally, making accurate flood magnitude estimation crucial. However, limited high-flow observations can affect the skill of conceptual rainfall-runoff models for flood simulations. Data resampling techniques have shown improved model calibration for imbalanced datasets, but do not preserve the temporal structure of time series data needed to correctly represent flood behaviour in conceptual models. Here this issue is addressed by developing a modified Kling-Gupta efficiency (KGE) objective function incorporating resampling methods, specifically over-sampling (OS) and hybrid-sampling (HS). We calibrated the Genie Rural a 4 Parameters Journalier (GR4J) model and the Sacramento Soil Moisture Accounting Model (SAC) across 168 Australian catchments, focusing on flows exceeding the 99.8th percentile. Compared to standard KGE and two high-flow objective functions (i.e., Nash-Sutcliffe Efficiency with a penalty term and Annual Peak Flow Bias) our resampling-modified KGE improved high-flow estimation in most catchments, particularly those with lower baseflow indices and higher runoff skewness.
