AB  - Accurate representation of multivariate dependence is essential in environmental stochastic simulation, yet increasing model flexibility often entails substantial computational cost and structural uncertainty. This study evaluates three Copula-based frameworks of increasing complexity: Gaussian/t Copulas (GtC), Dependence Tree Copulas (DTC), and Bayesian model averaging with vine Copulas (BMA-Vine). Using synthetic datasets and hydrological observations, model performance is assessed through correlation reconstruction and multivariate goodness-of-fit (GoF), including the Cramér–von Mises (CvM) statistic and energy distance (ED2). Results show that increased complexity does not consistently ensure superior performance across all data conditions. GtC remains stable for near-elliptical dependence, while DTC offers efficient sparse approximations but may underestimate indirect dependencies. BMA-Vine provides the most robust results, particularly for heterogeneous or tail-dependent structures. Findings suggest model selection should balance flexibility, data characteristics, and computational constraints rather than defaulting to complex vine constructions.
