AB  - Dynamically presenting the progression of flash floods within virtual geographical scenes enhances users' visual perception of flash flood hazards and decision-making efficacy. Existing flash flood simulation methods suffer from slow visual response and difficulties in online service delivery. We propose an online chained service framework for flash flood visual simulation, which integrates data-model-knowledge. First, we build a large language model (LLM)-driven integrated data-model-knowledge correlation framework. Second, we construct knowledge graph-guided construction of online service chain for flash floods simulation. Finally, we developed a B/S-based prototype system and validated it through case studies. The results demonstrate that the proposed framework effectively facilitates online simulation and dynamic rendering of flood evolution. Compared with conventional methods, the average service construction efficiency is improved by 54%. The scene rendering frame rate remains stable above 85 FPS, supporting dynamic queries for critical hydrological information. This method establishes a new paradigm for disaster virtual geographic scene services.
