AB  - The Mississippi River–Gulf of America continuum supports vital coastal communities and infrastructure but faces increasing exposure to compound coastal hazards. Forecasting across this land–coast–ocean system remains challenging due to complex interactions among hydrological, oceanic, and atmospheric processes. We present the Gulf of America Coastal Hazards Forecast System (GAm-CHFS), an operational modeling framework that integrates land, river, and ocean dynamics through both one-way and dynamically two-way coupling within the COAWST system. This framework enables seamless simulation of watershed–coastal interactions and resolves multiscale processes governing compound flooding, water quality, and biogeochemical dynamics. GAm-CHFS delivers 72-h forecasts of river discharge, coastal water levels, ocean circulation, hypoxia, and carbon fluxes across the Mississippi River Basin and adjacent coastal ocean. Case studies show improved prediction of nonlinear interactions among storm surge, rainfall, and river discharge. This system provides a foundation for AI-enabled, scalable, and uncertainty-aware coastal hazard forecasting.
