AB  - Land use changes shape terrestrial carbon budget, and accurate predictions of land use and associated carbon dynamics are essential for achieving carbon neutrality. This study develops a BN-PLUS-IC model that integrates a Bayesian network (BN) for probabilistic land use demand prediction, the Patch-Generating Land Use Simulation (PLUS) model for gridded patch level spatial allocation, and the IPCC carbon emission coefficient (IC) method for spatially explicit territorial carbon accounting. Applied to Fujian Province, China, under three SSPs (SSP119, SSP245, SSP585) and three GCMs (EC-Earth3-China, GFDL-ESM4-China, MRI-ESM2-China), BN-PLUS-IC yields a FOM of 0.19, PA of 0.36, and UA of 0.22 for BN, and a Kappa coefficient of 0.84 and OA of 0.89 for PLUS on the 2020 reference data. Across the scenario ensemble, construction land, water area, grassland, and bare land generally increase, while forest and cultivated land decline, with urbanization pressure propagating from coastal to inland regions. Net carbon emissions rise from [24.92, 25.79] × 106 t in 2030 to [25.61, 28.50] × 106 t in 2060, with variance decomposition revealing that SSPs dominate long term emissions (84.3-96.5%) while interaction between GCM and SSP dominates in 2030 (62.7%). Sensitivity analysis further identifies the construction land carbon emission coefficient as the single most influential IC parameter. These findings suggest that realizing Fujian's dual carbon goals requires reining in coastal construction land expansion, scaling up blue carbon and forest sink protection inland, and cross-departmental coordination among natural resources, ecology, and forestry agencies.
