AB  - Global Sensitivity Analysis (GSA) is a powerful tool for understanding the influence of input variables on model outputs, particularly in complex systems. This paper presents a practical approach for estimating Sobol’ indices and Shapley effects using random sampling high dimensional model representations (RS-HDMR) and relevance vector machine (RVM) regression. The proposed method leverages the properties of RS-HDMR to construct high-fidelity surrogate models that enable algebraic computation of Sobol’ indices and Shapley effects, complementing direct estimation approaches by providing a reusable approximation with interpretable component function decomposition. By mapping RS-HDMR expansion coefficients to Sobol’ indices and Shapley effects, the methodology provides a comprehensive framework for variance based GSA in complex systems. The integration of RVM regression enhances the accuracy and efficiency of estimating RS-HDMR expansion coefficients, making the approach suitable for high-dimensional data. The efficacy of the proposed method is demonstrated through a series of case studies, including the decomposition of benchmark analytic functions and the parameter sensitivity analysis of water quality models. The results indicate that while Sobol’ indices are robust for independent inputs, Shapley effects offer a more nuanced understanding in scenarios with interdependent variables. This dual approach provides a useful utility for practitioners, enabling informed decision-making under uncertainty.
ER  - 

