AB  - Most reservoirs remain poorly sampled of their actual capacity due to the high costs and logistical demands of conventional sedimentation surveys. Here, we present RECLAIM (Reservoir Estimation of Capacity Loss using AI-based Methods), a globally scalable framework to predict absolute sedimentation rates using observed records and multi-decadal Earth observations from satellite data (1984-2023). Using a dataset of 1315 observations with 88 static and dynamic catchment, climatic, and reservoir-specific features, RECLAIM integrates gradient boosting models in a dynamic ensemble, achieving a prediction RMSE of 4.44 Million Cubic Meter (MCM)/year, MAE of 1.65 MCM/year, and R2 = 0.86 on the fixed test set. Cross-validation across 10 data partitions yields RMSE = 7.62 ± 4.86 MCM/year and MAE = 3.13 ± 1.66 MCM/year, confirming stable generalization across diverse sedimentation regimes. The development of RECLAIM revealed that absolute sedimentation rate is a more robust predictand than the widely used capacity loss rate, which loses sedimentation physics when normalized by reservoir capacity. The use of RECLAIM is expected to support the long-term sustainability of reservoir infrastructure globally.
ER  - 

