AB  - We present an open-source Python pipeline to automate landscape modeling for hydrological analysis, addressing the limitations of manual GIS processing. The proposed reproducible GIS-to-graph workflow transforms geospatial land-use data into a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) to map functional connectivity. The core contribution integrates three sequential phases: data cleaning, structural consolidation of patch-boundary attributes, and topological generation. Nodes represent discrete soil patches, while directed edges represent topographic boundaries oriented by gravitational potential. The framework implements spatial graph algorithms—including degree centrality, Breadth-First Search, and an adapted Bellman–Ford procedure to identify potential runoff source areas and critical connectivity corridors within the model’s topological assumptions. Edges are weighted using a composite metric integrating elevation difference, land-cover contrast, and boundary length. A case study involving 1427 patches demonstrates high computational efficiency with O(|V|+|E|) complexity for path identification. This scalable software solution bridges computer science methodology and geoscientific applications, providing a robust tool for precision environmental management and runoff mitigation planning.
