AB  - Traditional regulation of open-channel flow has primarily emphasized water levels, although flow velocity is equally important for sediment transport and aquatic environments. To enable real-time regulation of both variables, this study proposes a physics-informed predictive control framework that integrates hydrodynamic principles with data-driven learning for real-time multi-variable regulation. A control-embedded physics-informed neural networks framework is developed for rapid and physically consistent prediction of hydrodynamic states, and is coupled with model predictive control for efficient constrained optimization. HEC-RAS is adopted for accurate state initialization. Numerical results show that the proposed model achieves higher prediction accuracy than purely data-driven surrogates, particularly in data-scarce cases, while realizing coordinated water-level tracking, avoiding low-velocity stagnation, and substantially reducing online computational cost. The framework provides insights into the development of efficient, physics-informed predictive control strategies for real-time multi-variable regulation of open-channel and riverine flow systems.
