AB  - Real-time air quality monitoring is crucial for pollution control and public health, yet sparse station distribution limits spatial coverage. Existing deep learning models typically focus on single pollutants and fail to jointly capture spatiotemporal continuity, local heterogeneity, and causal mechanisms. To address this, we propose CausalHMoE-STFNN, a two-stage multi-pollutant causal inference model. Stage I builds a Causal Encoder with Hybrid Mixture of Experts (CausalHMoE), where a Causal Encoder models causal relations between local features and pollutants, and a Hybrid Mixture of Experts captures inter-pollutant interactions for multi-task initial prediction. Stage II employs a short-term Spatio-Temporal Field Neural Network (STFNN) to model the continuous evolution of air quality and refine inference using Stage I outputs. Experiments conducted on the Chongqing and Beijing datasets demonstrate that the proposed model achieves the best or near-best performance across most pollutants and evaluation metrics. Compared with the strongest baseline model, CausalHMoE-STFNN reduces the Overall RMSE by 10.18% and 21.86% on the Chongqing and Beijing datasets, respectively, while the Overall MAE is reduced by 13.08% and 8.14%, confirming its accuracy, component effectiveness, and interpretability.
