AB  - The identification of anomalies within time series is a mature field. This paper, however, introduces a distinct methodology tailored for detecting extremely subtle anomalies in data heavily corrupted by significant noise and trends originating from non-polynomial and nonlinear dynamics. Our innovative technique utilizes a tailored Continuous Wavelet Transform (CWT) based filtering pipeline to deconstruct data and effectively remove nonlinear and non-polynomial trends, subsequently leading to precise identification of anomalies using Local Active Information Storage (LAIS) as an unsupervised anomaly indicator. Our analysis of the LAIS scores makes it possible to detect anomalies. More specifically, this kind of analysis, when applied to steady pressures inside a Water Distribution Network (WDN), allows us to identify very tiny incipient leaks. We successfully tested our method on publicly available realistic synthetic data, pertaining to Hanoi WDN, containing minute leaks that other forefront methods available in literature were unable to detect.
