AB  - The Segura River Basin in southeastern Spain relies on the Tagus–Segura water transfer for agricultural activities. Reliable one-to three-month and seasonal reservoir inflow forecasts are essential for managing transfers from the Upper Tagus River Basin reservoirs (Entrepeñas and Buendía). This study develops a seasonal hydrological forecasting framework using ECMWF's SEAS5 ensemble climate predictions. Bias-corrected forecasts with machine learning generate daily precipitation and temperatures up to seven months ahead. These drive a calibrated SWAT+ model to simulate reservoir inflows. Model calibration (2000-2009) and validation (2010-2019) showed strong performance (NSE >0.7, PBIAS <14%). Bias-corrected forecasts enabled skilful inflow predictions, with high reliability at 1–3 months (NSE = 0.91–0.73) and seasonal scales. Performance declines toward 7 months (NSE = 0.58), although CRPSS remains generally positive, outperforming traditional estimates calculated from historical data. This demonstrates the value of integrating seasonal climate forecasts into operational water-resource decision-making in drought-prone basins.
