AB  - Regulated river networks exhibit strongly nonstationary nutrient dynamics driven by time-varying hydrologic connectivity and gate operations, which challenges stable multi-horizon forecasting of Total Nitrogen (TN) and Total Phosphorus (TP). We develop the Dynamic Edge Weight and Virtual-node Spatio-Temporal Multi-Variable Gate-control Graph Neural Network (DEWV-STMV-G), a control-aware spatiotemporal graph learning framework for nutrient prediction in gate-regulated river networks. The model integrates node-wise sequential encoding for short-term hydrology–water-quality memory, basin-scale meteorology–calendar encoding for exogenous forcing and periodicity, and explicit gate-control conditioning to represent operation-dependent regime shifts. The river system is represented as a transport-consistent dynamic directed weighted graph, where edge directions follow instantaneous flow signs and edge weights vary with transport intensity and distance decay. To preserve topological closure under sparse monitoring, unobserved confluences are completed through virtual-node estimation before graph propagation. DEWV-STMV-G directly predicts TN and TP at 3, 6, 12, and 24 h horizons without recursive rollout. Using hourly observations from August 1, 2023 to September 22, 2025 and a time-ordered 80/20 train–test split, DEWV-STMV-G achieved robust multi-horizon prediction performance. On the test set, TN R2 decreased from 0.9665 at 3 h to 0.9035 at 24 h, while TP R2 decreased from 0.9665 to 0.8328, with MAE and RMSE increasing as lead time grew. The model outperformed representative spatiotemporal graph baselines and ablated variants, demonstrating the value of jointly modeling dynamic connectivity, virtual-node completion, exogenous drivers, and regulation-aware conditioning. Stratified evaluation showed that gate-opening periods were the main source of long-horizon uncertainty, whereas steady periods retained high predictive skill at 24 h. External validation in an independent regulated river network further showed averaged R2 values of 0.8250 for TN and 0.8725 for TP, providing preliminary evidence of cross-basin transferability. These results suggest that DEWV-STMV-G can support short-term forecasting and early-warning applications in regulated river-network water-quality management.
