AB  - The interactions between socio-economic and water resources subsystems are increasingly significant under climate change and intense human activities. Research on the evolution of future water systems is essential to understand regional water sustainability. However, traditional socio-hydrological systems normally focus on the response of the water resources system to external disturbances, while the factors of water resources availability on socio-economic development are represented in a limited way. In this paper, a novel socio-hydrological model, constructed by coupling a distributed hydrological model and a system dynamics model, has been proposed. The coupled models simulate dynamic feedbacks between socio-economic and water resources systems. Specifically, increasing water stress triggers five negative feedback pathways that limit social-economic development, and lower the water supply-demand rodio, thereby reducing social water demand. Through a case study of the Weihe River Basin in China, where the model achieved an NSE of 0.9 for streamflow during the validation period, results reveal that industrial production is more sensitive to changes in water resources than population and irrigation. Future water availability can be overestimated (up to 3%) if neglecting the influence of socio-economic development on water resources; while ignoring the impact of water resources on socio-economic development can lead to an underestimation of water availability by 7.8%. The model provides an online tool for coupled system simulation and offers insights into system feedbacks and coevolution under climate change and socioeconomic development.
