AB  - Deciphering current extreme rainfall patterns through the retrieval of historical counterparts supports contextual rainfall assessment and hydrometeorological modelling. However, existing methods still struggle to capture the pronounced temporal variability and irregular spatial organization of extreme rainfall patterns, and their limited scalability hinders adaptation to growing rainfall archives. This study proposes a novel method for rainfall analog retrieval at hourly, daily, and event scales, integrating a vision transformer with masked pretraining to learn rainfall representations, an intensity-guided loss to embed hydrologic priors and emphasize extremes, and a spatiotemporal evaluation system for rainfall event evolution. Experiments demonstrate accurate and efficient retrieval of diverse rainfall patterns, with a mean query time of 21.35 ms over more than 10000 rainfall maps. The label-free method scales to expanding rainfall archives and retrieves dynamic analogues for localized extremes and rare large-scale events, extending retrieval from static pattern matching to dynamic event tracking.
