AB  - Aggregate probabilistic scores are central to evaluating forecast quality but often obscure spatiotemporal variability in uncertainty. We propose a diagnostic framework for spatiotemporal probabilistic forecasts that complements standard metrics with a visual grammar linking geographic maps and polar representations. Spatial order is fixed, diagnostic magnitude encoded radially, and forecast horizons shown as concentric rings, enabling joint assessment of interval width, interval exceedance, horizon-dependent drift, and quantile structure. We apply the framework to annual land-subsidence forecasts in Zhongshan, China, comparing the eXtreme Temporal Fusion Transformer (XTFT), Quantile Gradient Boosting Machine (QGBM), and Quantile Autoregression (QAR). Results show that XTFT is closest to nominal coverage with ordered quantiles, while QGBM and QAR exhibit under-coverage. Spatial diagnostics further reveal that similar median fields can mask substantial differences in uncertainty geometry, interval exceedance, and distributional spread. The model-agnostic framework supports interpretation, comparison, and monitoring of spatial probabilistic forecasts.
