AB  - Spatial models used in environmental assessment are often used to predict concentrations, even when the underlying decision problem concerns the delineation of threshold exceedance areas. We show that the common practice of thresholding point predictions can introduce substantial bias in estimated exceedance areas. We propose a decision-aligned modeling and evaluation workflow that frames exceedance mapping as a probabilistic prediction task. The approach calibrates exceedance maps to match an unbiased area estimate and assesses model performance using target-weighted cross-validation. In a groundwater-nitrate case study, several widely used interpolation and machine-learning models underestimate exceedance area by 80%–98% when exceedance areas are derived by thresholding concentration predictions, despite competitive concentration-based error metrics. Area calibration removes this bias and yields a balanced trade-off between omission and commission. The workflow is model-agnostic and compatible with standard geostatistical and machine-learning methods, providing a general framework for task-aligned modeling and uncertainty quantification in environmental applications involving threshold-based decisions.
