AB  - Water quality modeling (WQM) faces enormous uncertainties due principally to process complexity representation and data limitations. Its uncertainties can never be entirely eliminated but can be managed by an uncertainty assessment (UA) approach that enhances confidence in both modeling process decisions and its outcomes. A paradigm shift from uncertainty analysis of a model to broader UA is vital for testing assumptions and evaluating model reliability from multiple methods, assumptions and perspectives. Reviewing the past decade of WQM papers reveals that attention to uncertainty is not routine. When it occurs, coverage of uncertainty is strongest for traditional sources: data, parametric and structural. We recommend broader integration of methods, including scaling comparisons, cross-validation, graphical and statistical performance evaluation, scenario analysis of non-probabilistic assumptions, explanation of choices, documentation to record assumptions and model evaluation exercises, and improved communication with interest groups. Progress also depends on computational efficiencies to explore assumptions more completely.
ER  - 

