AB  - Hourly flood forecasting is critical for disaster mitigation, yet methodological inconsistencies in input architectures and optimization algorithms often undermine comparisons between Process-Based Models (PBMs) and Deep Learning Models (DLMs). This study presents a systematic evaluation framework that standardizes these elements to ensure an equitable comparison of PBM and DLM in hourly flood simulation. We established a comparative framework incorporating four representative models: the Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) and Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) for DLMs, alongside the HBV and Tank models for PBMs. Applied across seven Chinese catchments, our investigation yielded principal findings: (1) The GRU model, when fed with the complete flood process as input, achieved superior simulation accuracy compared to using sliding-window samples; (2) Adam is an effective method for parameter calibration within PBMs; (3) Under unified input and optimization conditions, DLMs generally achieved superior simulation performance to PBMs across most catchments.
