AB  - R- Trees and R-Tree variants have been widely adopted as the spatial indexing structures in the underlying databases of major Geographic Information Systems (GIS). However, most traditional R-Tree variants rely on handcrafted heuristic rules or parameters derived from expert knowledge, often facing performance limitations with locally optimal structures. In this paper, we introduce the Global Structure-Aware R-Tree (GSAR-Tree), an approach for constructing an R-Tree variant using deep reinforcement learning (DRL) with a self-play mechanism. Our approach replaces the traditional heuristic rules with the RL agent's choices, yielding more informed and globally optimized insertion decisions. We performed a series of experiments leveraging multiple synthetic datasets with varying distributions alongside real-world OpenStreetMap (OSM) data, and evaluated query efficiency under different data volumes, training-set ratios, and query sizes. Experimental results show that our proposed GSAR-Tree outperforms R∗-Tree, achieving a reduction in node accesses ranging from 37.9% to 1193.8%.
